2186547972 #edb/ref 1071 "${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547972 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0515d331-a2ed-5378-ab7b-e5eca8ff6d75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547972 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $3.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{3},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{4}{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{3}=\\frac{4}{9}(\\frac{27}{64})=\\frac{3}{16} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{4}{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{4}=\\frac{4}{9}(\\frac{81}{256})=\\frac{9}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{(\\frac{9}{64})}{(\\frac{3}{16})}=\\frac{9}{64}\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{16})}{(1-\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{16})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548001 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548001 #edb/ref 1069 "q-350641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548001 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548001 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2n}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2n}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548001 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c0fb001-b215-5979-89f5-05a01ae52266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548001 #edb/ref 1074 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2(1)}=\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2(2)}=\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}}{\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}={(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{1}{9})}{(1-\\frac{1}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(\\frac{8}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{9}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548019 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548019 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124590" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548019 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548019 #edb/ref 1071 "${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{25}{27}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548019 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ac43aed-fdf1-5439-85aa-b688b91d01e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548019 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $3.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{3},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{25}{27}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{25}{27}(\\frac{27}{125})=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{25}{27}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{4}=\\frac{25}{27}(\\frac{81}{625})=\\frac{3}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{25})}{(\\frac{1}{5})}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{25}{27}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5})}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548022 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548022 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548022 #edb/ref 1071 "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548022 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "84522aa8-0251-51f3-8f96-c1848baee4b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548022 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}=5.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}{10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548047 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548047 #edb/ref 1069 "q-350640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548047 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548047 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2n}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2n}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548047 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b85d1cfe-34fb-5d55-ad84-699fc2c94f6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548047 #edb/ref 1074 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2(1)}=\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2(2)}=\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{4}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2}}={(-0.4)}^{2}=0.16.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2}}{1-0.16} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}(0.16)}{0.84} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.4}{0.84} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548086 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "120cee62-a2ba-56d3-bd5a-73197dd03830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548086 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186548001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548128 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186548173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548128 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8661678d-4c4b-54f6-b95b-86b213e25dda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548128 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186548022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548156 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186548086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548156 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0968cfd-c4e6-58c4-bbe3-c52236c84b10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548156 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186548047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548173 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186548177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548173 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "da72f953-d706-5801-a724-042fb2b1439b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548173 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186547972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548177 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186548156 #e